Annex A — Complete scheme definition

aceup: ACE User Profile URI Scheme
Version: 0.5-draft
Edition: ace-profile-family-2026-10-14-r4
Revised: 14 October 2026. For IANA review; registration pending.
Canonical form: aceup:<opaque-id>
Permanent edition: https://seqentra.com/specifications/uri/aceup/0.5-draft-r4
Permanent machine edition: https://seqentra.com/specifications/uri/aceup/0.5-draft-r4.json
Edition integrity: https://seqentra.com/specifications/uri/aceup/0.5-draft-r4/integrity.json

Purpose and status

A persistent identifier for a governed user profile under the ACE profile contract defined here. The profile is a governed descriptive record associated with a human participant; it is not the human, an account or an authenticated principal. It is independent of email, telephone number, username, device, provider and network location, and is not a self-relative reference to the current user.

The profile is the identified resource. Its subject, controller, issuer, representations and endpoints are separate concepts. A subject binding is a claim requiring independent validation; this specification does not infer subject identity or equivalence from matching tokens, display names or profile content.

This experimental proposal is intended for exchange between independent implementations beyond one organisation. The implementation is a local parser and read-only evidence verifier. Production allocation, network resolution, broad adoption and completed independent interoperability are not claimed. No Seqentra account or mandatory Seqentra service is required.

ACE names this profile contract. It does not amend or extend the registered ace: URI scheme and does not inherit that registration. aceup, aceep, acelp, aceap and acedp are five separate proposed schemes; each requires its own IANA assessment. The original requests were submitted to IANA. Under request #1461251, IANA suggested the more descriptive names aceup, aceep, acelp, aceap and acedp. These documents incorporate the requested naming revision for continued review. Publication does not establish registration or approval.

Publication and implementation scope

Implementation evidence in the dated 0.5-draft-r3 companion and its signed test vectors concerns the earlier three-letter scheme names. This publication changes the proposed scheme literals and review documents only; it does not claim that runtime support for the revised names has been deployed or independently verified. The historical signed examples are not relabelled and do not validate a renamed URI.

Syntax

The syntax is an absolute RFC 3986 URI with a rootless path used as a single opaque identifier. It has no URI authority and no hierarchical semantics. A token has 1–128 ASCII characters, begins with an ASCII letter or digit, and then permits only letters, digits, period, underscore, tilde and hyphen. Class prefixes and slashes are not supported. Percent escapes, user information, ports, queries, fragments, whitespace and non-ASCII characters are refused, not repaired.

The scheme is ASCII case-insensitive; canonical output lowercases only the scheme. Identifier bytes are case-sensitive and are never Unicode-normalized, decoded, lowercased or interpreted as a host. No additional IRI syntax is defined. The quoted ABNF literal is case-insensitive under RFC 5234; ALPHA and DIGIT are its core rules.

aceup-uri = "aceup:" opaque-id
opaque-id = ( ALPHA / DIGIT ) *127( ALPHA / DIGIT / "." / "_" / "~" / "-" )

Meaning and equality

An identifier denotes one governed user profile record. Equality compares the complete canonical URI within this contract. Matching tokens under different schemes do not identify the same profile or subject. A profile is distinct from a security principal; possessing or parsing its URI grants no authentication, consent, capability, authority or access.

A profile identifier can remain stable across properly admitted revisions of that same profile. An endpoint, display name, key or representation change does not by itself establish a new profile. A change of subject or profile kind must not silently repurpose an existing identifier; issue a new identifier and review any relationship separately.

Personhood, account ownership, consent and control of a human subject are not established by profile metadata or a valid signature. A subject can have multiple context-specific profiles without any automatic link between them.

Resolution and evidence

This draft defines identification and local read-only verification, not a network protocol. Evidence is accepted only for the exact canonical URI, internal profile ace-profile:profile:user, supported version 0.5-draft and an independently configured key scoped to that profile. The JSON representation must contain profile_kind equal to user; that member is signed. The previous principal_kind field is not a substitute. The internal ace-profile label is not a registered URI scheme.

The verifier checks a closed evidence envelope, Ed25519 signature, validity interval, explicit revocation status, unambiguous record selection and the signed profile kind before returning an immutable representation. Missing, stale, revoked, conflicting, wrong-kind, unsupported or unverifiable evidence is refused. A valid signature authenticates evidence under configured trust, not the subject, issuer entitlement or profile controller.

The representation is an inert JSON object. Apart from profile_kind, extension members have no authority semantics in this base contract and are not followed, executed or interpreted as verified subject bindings. Application profiles must specify and independently validate any subject, country-code, ownership, control or delegation claims they consume.

The URI alone must not cause a network request, endpoint invocation, model execution, device control, mutation or disclosure. The local candidate performs no network discovery even when network use is requested. No default resolver or automatic provider fallback is defined; production access and disclosure policies remain separate work.

The companion ACE Profile Local Evidence Format, edition 0.5-draft-r3, documents the exact record and configured-key fields, signing bytes, signature encoding, validity boundaries, resource limits and synthetic verification vectors used by this experimental local implementation. It is available at https://seqentra.com/specifications/uri/profile-evidence/0.5-draft-r3. This is an implementation contract for local API objects, not a network interchange protocol, JSON canonicalization standard or production trust framework. Applications must not invent a wire protocol from these examples; nested member order and runtime date parsing are explicit interoperability limitations.

Allocation and lifecycle

The token is opaque and must not encode credentials, contact details or account/provider routing. The bounded grammar is not an allocator. Any production allocator must establish uniqueness across participating issuers, sufficient entropy, collision detection, issuer accountability, non-reassignment and independently reviewed admission. Cross-issuer uniqueness and a global allocation service are not implemented.

Use context-specific profiles where needed to limit correlation. Recovery, delegation, key rotation, revocation, succession and cross-context linking require independently admitted evidence. Refuse namespace collisions instead of guessing a provider. Revocation or deletion never frees an identifier for a different profile or subject.

Earlier aup: version 0.4 identified a user principal, not a profile. No production identifiers were issued by this project under that experimental draft. A 0.5 allocator MUST NOT reuse any earlier aup token for a profile; historical identifiers retain their original denotation. Fresh allocation and collision checks against retained history are required. Version numbers and new internal profile IDs alone do not make reuse safe. No allocator or automatic conversion is provided here. Production issuance must remain disabled until an allocator can enforce historical non-reuse and independently admitted subject bindings. The local conflict check is not a global allocation guarantee.

Security and privacy

Opaque identifiers can remain correlatable and are not secrets or bearer credentials. Minimize collection, logging and disclosure. The local test verifier distinguishes unknown resources for debugging and must not be exposed as a public private-identity lookup service; a production protocol needs an explicit privacy-preserving refusal policy.

Do not embed tokens, passwords, keys or sensitive personal data. Do not use an untrusted endpoint to bypass network/origin restrictions. Signature verification, HTTPS, a registry listing or registration number alone cannot establish control or access. Optional unsigned read receipts are not authority attestations. Mutations and execution are not implemented.

Interoperability and migration

The 14 October 2026 revision changes the proposed names aup, aep, alp, aap and adp to aceup, aceep, acelp, aceap and acedp respectively. Their user, enterprise, location, agent and device profile meanings are unchanged. HTTP redirects between specification pages are document navigation only; they do not equate identifiers. Existing identifiers, stored keys, signatures, receipts and bindings retain their original text and meaning. No automatic conversion or cross-scheme equivalence is defined. The following earlier migration history remains applicable to its original editions.

Version 0.5-draft is a semantic revision, not a spelling-only rename. The aup:/aca: 0.4 principal drafts are preserved at /specifications/uri/aup/0.4-draft and /specifications/uri/aca/0.4-draft with matching .json editions. The 0.5-draft-r3 aup page describes User Profile; aca is superseded by the aap Agent Profile document. Document succession does not equate the old principal with a new profile.

Earlier me:/ai:, usr:/agt: version 0.2, gup:/gca: version 0.3 and aup:/aca: version 0.4 records are not converted, aliased or relabelled. Preserve stored identifiers, primary keys, signatures, receipts, cached bindings and trust anchors. A profile about an old principal requires a fresh profile identifier, explicit separately validated subject binding and newly signed 0.5 evidence; there is no conversion service.

The local verifier refuses unsupported versions and wrong internal profile IDs. Even where URI text starts with aup, previous signed principal evidence cannot satisfy the profile contract. Legacy me:self/... and me_uri fields, au:me/... country classes, and existing ace://ai/... or ace://agent/... resources retain their historical meanings.

Existing au:/us:/nz:/ca:/gb: country-context experiments remain historical local contracts, not aliases of alp. No country-specific schemes are being applied for in this round. The registered ace: syntax, CRI 26 and discovery contract remain unchanged. No new /.well-known suffix, general URI handler or public resolver activation is requested.

Edition 0.5-draft-r3 replaces the then-current ACP Country Profile proposal with ALP Location Profile. The 7 October ACP definitions, identifiers and signed evidence retain their historical country-profile meaning. acp: is not an alias of alp:, and no prefix, profile-kind or signature conversion is permitted. The 0.5-draft-r3 local candidate refuses acp:; historical evidence must be interpreted under its original contract. The other four profile kinds retain their existing meaning and signing format.

Naming, alternatives and limitations

The proposed name aceup expands to ACE User Profile and selects the narrow governed user profile contract defined here. The ACE?P pattern is a naming convention, not a reserved prefix, registration of a scheme family or automatic right to future names. It claims no universal or standard status. Five characters alone do not establish compliance with RFC 7595 section 3.8; descriptiveness, distinguishability and rights to use the name remain for IANA assessment.

Prior-name review retained from the 8 October edition: AUP is already used for Agent UI Protocol in the pinned AIGNE AFS README and Acceptable Use Policy in Internet usage. This proposal defines neither of those concepts and claims no compatibility, affiliation or succession. These prior associations remain material to RFC 7595 section 3.8.

HTTPS identifiers with indirection, a URN namespace, an appropriate existing identifier scheme or typed profiles within one shared scheme can express comparable resources. Type selection before dereferencing is the proposed benefit, enabling a consumer to select a specific profile validator. Role separation, transport independence and a consistent naming pattern alone do not prove that five global names are necessary. That benefit and the cost of separate schemes remain for review; broad community support and international SDO sponsorship are not asserted.

IANA’s correspondence under request #1460930 closed the earlier me/ai naming request and invited other names with the RFC 7595 template and an explicit CRI answer. It is not approval of this family. The intermediate usr/agt, gup/gca and aup/aca principal drafts were never submitted; no IANA decision on those drafts is claimed.

The more descriptive name aceup was proposed in IANA correspondence under request #1461251. The naming revision does not alter the profile scope or assert completed IANA approval.

CRI requirement

A CRI scheme number in the 0–999 range is not required for this experimental profile. No specific number or special allocation is requested; ordinary IANA assignment is sufficient. The implementation defines no constrained CRI wire codec and provides no measured requirement for a scarce low-range number. Any later special request needs a concrete technical case and separate expert review. URI registration and numerical allocation are distinct; existing ace: CRI 26 is unchanged.

