Annex B — Experimental local evidence implementation contract

ACE Profile Local Evidence Format
Version: 0.5-draft
Edition: 0.5-draft-r3
Permanent reference: https://seqentra.com/specifications/uri/profile-evidence/0.5-draft-r3

Scope and compatibility

This companion documents the experimental LocalProfileResolver evidence contract used by the current five ACE profile URI schemes at version 0.5-draft. Edition 0.5-draft-r3 selects ALP Location Profile in place of the previous ACP Country Profile. ALP has a distinct URI scheme, signed location kind and profile-specific key scope; acp: is not accepted by the current candidate and is never converted to alp:. The record structure, signature bytes and meanings of the other four profile kinds remain unchanged. It does not create a production resolver, allocation service, network protocol or trust in a profile subject. Signed records retain specification_version 0.5-draft.
The verifier accepts already-constructed inert ECMAScript data values and independently configured local keys. It is not a JSON text parser. JSON source text, UTF-8 transport decoding and duplicate-member handling are separate ingestion concerns. No interoperable network exchange format is claimed by this local contract. A future network format must define and test those boundaries explicitly rather than treating a schema as a complete parser or trust policy.

Closed evidence record

A record is an object with exactly these nine own enumerable data members: canonical_uri, profile_id, specification_version, representation, issued_at, expires_at, key_id, revoked and signature. No members are optional and no additional envelope members are allowed. All except representation and revoked are nonempty strings. revoked is a Boolean. The representation is an inert JSON object with an own profile_kind member equal to the scheme kind. Additional representation members are permitted as inert signed data; they convey no authority semantics in this base contract.
The exact bindings are aup / ace-profile:profile:user / user; aep / ace-profile:profile:enterprise / enterprise; alp / ace-profile:profile:location / location; aap / ace-profile:profile:agent / agent; and adp / ace-profile:profile:device / device. canonical_uri must equal the requested URI after lowercasing its scheme only. A stored uppercase scheme does not match that canonical URI. specification_version must equal 0.5-draft. The internal profile label is a configuration label, not a registered URI scheme.

Independent local key configuration

A key entry is an object with exactly six own enumerable data members: key_id, profile_id, public_key_pem, valid_from, valid_until and revoked. The first five are nonempty strings; revoked is a Boolean. Exactly one key entry must match both the record key_id and the selected internal profile_id. A duplicate key entry is a verification failure even if its contents match.
The key is supplied by trusted local configuration, not derived from the URI or admitted merely because it accompanies a record. public_key_pem is parsed by the implementation using Node.js createPublicKey; the resulting asymmetric key type must be ed25519. Public SubjectPublicKeyInfo PEM is the portable example form used here. The local implementation also accepts other PEM forms that that API can read into an Ed25519 public key; it does not establish an issuer-entitlement policy or a global trust-distribution format.

Signed payload and signature encoding

Construct a new object whose members occur in this exact order: canonical_uri, profile_id, specification_version, representation, issued_at, expires_at, key_id, revoked. Each value is copied from the verified inert snapshot. Serialize this object with ECMAScript JSON.stringify with no replacer and no space argument. Encode the resulting string as UTF-8, without a byte order mark or trailing newline. Those bytes are the message signed and verified. The signature member is excluded. No prehash, context string or additional domain prefix is applied.
Nested representation objects retain ECMAScript property enumeration semantics: array-index property names appear in ascending numeric order and remaining string property names retain creation order; arrays retain element order. JSON.stringify supplies its standard string escaping and finite Number serialization. This is not JSON Canonicalization Scheme (JCS) and does not sort arbitrary object members. Reordering non-index representation members can invalidate a signature even if an application considers the resulting JSON objects equivalent. A producer in another language must reproduce the exact serialized bytes, including numeric and Unicode string handling.
Use Ed25519 (the pure Ed25519 variant, not Ed25519ph or Ed25519ctx). The 64 signature octets are encoded with the URL-safe base64 alphabet, without padding. The result must be exactly 86 characters from A-Z, a-z, 0-9, hyphen and underscore. Decoding and canonical unpadded base64url re-encoding must reproduce the supplied signature exactly; this excludes noncanonical unused pad bits. Whitespace, padding and other characters are refused.

const message = JSON.stringify({
  canonical_uri: record.canonical_uri,
  profile_id: record.profile_id,
  specification_version: record.specification_version,
  representation: record.representation,
  issued_at: record.issued_at,
  expires_at: record.expires_at,
  key_id: record.key_id,
  revoked: record.revoked
});
// UTF-8(message) is the Ed25519 message. No BOM or trailing newline.
// The record and configured key must pass every other check first.

Time, revocation and selection

issued_at, expires_at, valid_from and valid_until are parsed with ECMAScript Date.parse in the installed runtime. All four parsed values and the resolver clock must be finite. The verifier requires valid_from <= issued_at < expires_at <= valid_until and issued_at <= now < expires_at. Either record.revoked or key.revoked being true refuses resolution. Local configuration is resampled on each read; this provides no remote revocation freshness guarantee.
Portable producers should use real UTC timestamps in YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm:ss.sssZ form, as in the vectors. The 0.5 implementation does not enforce that spelling: Date.parse can accept additional formats or normalize inputs, and nonstandard forms may vary by runtime. Do not describe this existing acceptance rule as strict RFC 3339. Tightening timestamp acceptance requires a separately reviewed compatibility change.
Before choosing a record, retained evidence for the same canonical URI with another internal profile_id or specification_version causes PROFILE_HISTORY_CONFLICT. This conservative refusal does not authenticate the conflicting historical claim. Otherwise there must be exactly one record matching the canonical URI and profile_id: none yields UNKNOWN_RESOURCE and more than one yields AMBIGUOUS_RESOURCE, including duplicate copies. The native multi-source coordinator also refuses an incompatible retained profile/version returned by another local or cache source; independently verified same-version representations that disagree produce a conflict. This local history check is not a global non-reuse guarantee.

Inert snapshot and resource limits

Before cryptographic verification the record and selected key are separately copied and frozen. Allowed values are null, Boolean, string, finite ECMAScript Number, ordinary objects (Object.prototype or null prototype) and dense ordinary arrays. Getters, setters, functions, undefined, BigInt, symbols, custom prototypes, dates, maps, cycles, sparse arrays, symbol properties and non-enumerable data members are refused. No user serialization method is invoked. A property named __proto__ is preserved as an own data property, not applied as a prototype change.
For each complete record snapshot and each complete key snapshot independently, the existing implementation permits at most 10,000 visited values and a nesting depth of 64 with the root at depth 0. It charges UTF-8 bytes for every visited string value and own data member name, excluding the built-in array length property, refusing a sum greater than 1,048,576; this is not a bound on serialized JSON bytes. It also rejects an object with more than 10,001 own keys or an array length greater than 10,000, and arrays must have exactly their dense indexed members plus length. Implementations must not claim a different counting rule is byte-for-byte equivalent.

Result and assurance boundary

Success is RESOLVED_LOCAL and returns the isolated immutable representation with evidence_signature_verified true, subject_authenticated false and authority_granted false. It demonstrates verification under independently configured local key material. It does not establish personhood, agent autonomy, device ownership, enterprise standing, government endorsement, control, consent, access permission, safe execution, issuer entitlement or truth of representation claims.
Optional local read receipts are unsigned. Their result_digest is the lowercase hexadecimal SHA-256 digest of the exact signature message bytes; receipt_id is local-read: followed by that digest. Native-coordinator receipts use a separate digest construction and must not be confused with this LocalProfileResolver receipt. Neither receipt is an authority attestation. No URI or representation is followed, fetched or invoked by this verifier.

Companion schemas and test vectors

The accompanying record and key schemas express object shape, required types, scheme/kind bindings and version values. They do not establish member ordering, finite-runtime numeric fidelity, inert property descriptors, resource limits, date parsing or interval relationships, historical non-reuse, key admission, canonical signature pad bits or cryptographic validity. Those checks remain required by this local contract. The profile representation intentionally allows additional inert members.
All vector identifiers and key material are synthetic test fixtures, not issued profile identifiers or trust anchors. Each positive baseline vector includes the exact signature message as text and hexadecimal bytes, its SHA-256 digest, the complete signed record, independent local key entry and test clock. A vector key must never be used for production admission.

